From 02ba16d69db566dd36301f2d03d50b73d3d65cd8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 19:40:47 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Render on devices without dynamic rendering, and fix the
 assembly identity

The port refused to start on any adapter without VK_KHR_dynamic_rendering --
which is every Adreno 6xx and much of Mali -- because the RHI builds its whole
render path on vkCmdBeginRendering and throws on the first pipeline otherwise.

Three separate faults, each of which alone was enough to stop the game:

1. Assembly identity did not match what the native bridge asks for.
   jni_bridge.cpp resolves CoreCLR delegates against the assembly "OpenIGI.Android"
   and the type "OpenIGI.Android.AndroidHost", but the C# declares
   namespace ProjectIGI.Android, so every build produced assemblies the bridge
   could not find. The shipped APK only ran because assets/managed still held an
   older OpenIGI.* assembly set, so the ProjectIGI.* set was never loaded at all.
   Directory.Build.props now derives AssemblyName as OpenIGI.*, AndroidHost keeps
   the OpenIGI.Android namespace the ABI requires, and the CVar generator takes
   its namespace from RootNamespace rather than the assembly name so the
   generated registrations stay where their callers look for them.

2. No render pass path existed. VulkanRenderPassCache builds and caches
   VkRenderPass objects keyed by everything baked into one (formats, sample
   count, load/store ops, depth layout), plus framebuffers keyed by the views
   attached. The encoder branches at the single begin/end seam, and pipelines are
   built against a format-compatible pass when dynamic rendering is unavailable.
   This is sound because Vulkan render pass compatibility is decided by formats
   and sample counts alone (spec 8.2), not by load/store ops -- so a pipeline
   built against one pass is valid inside another with the same formats.

3. Framebuffers outlived the views they named. The engine wraps a swapchain image
   in a fresh texture every frame and disposes the previous one, so the driver
   hands the same image view handle straight back out. A framebuffer cache keyed
   by handle therefore hit on entries built from destroyed views, drawing into an
   image nobody presents -- visible as flicker and overlapping frames. Framebuffer
   reuse is now within a frame only, and each slot's framebuffers are destroyed
   once its fence has signalled, mirroring how frame textures are already retired.

The dynamic rendering path is untouched and still taken on every adapter that
supports it; this only adds the path the rest of the world needs.
